ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:223:| vetting completion | 1 | **hard prerequisite: M10 A3 ("vetting challenges survive serverless")** — challenges live in a process-local `Map` even in the DB store (`agents/db.ts:282`) and the route consumes the challenge *before* its durable writes, so a genuine Tier-1 action is unimplementable until challenges are DB rows; if Phase A hasn't landed by train a2, P1.4 absorbs A3 (recorded in the inventory, following the D5/C7 pattern). The action then runs one batch: durable conditional challenge consumption **stamping a request token** (`UPDATE … SET consumed = true, consumed_request = $req WHERE id = $c AND NOT consumed`) + vetted flip + **the two bootstrap evaluations with ensure-semantics, one CTE statement each** (fresh agents have no `poaw`/`identity-check` registrations — `evaluation_results.registration_id` is a required FK and today's route calls `registerForEvaluation` before each save, so a batch that ignores registrations fails for every new agent, while creating them outside the batch would reopen the partial-commit window — but unconditional inserts are just as wrong: agents can register for evaluations *before* vetting (the register route only authenticates), and the partial unique index on active registrations (`idx_eval_reg_active`) would 23505 an unconditional insert, aborting the whole batch and dead-ending vetting for a valid agent; today's route deliberately reuses an existing registration and skips an already-passed evaluation (`vetting/complete/route.ts`), so per evaluation one statement with the **already-passed check gating everything, registration included** (today's route checks `hasPassedEvaluation` *before* registering — a shape that gated only the result would hand an agent who passed long ago a fresh dangling `registered` row plus its `evaluation.registered` event, re-occupying `idx_eval_reg_active` against legitimate future registrations and reporting a phantom in-progress evaluation): where the evaluation is not already passed — insert the registration if no active one exists **directly in its terminal `completed` state** (a bootstrap registration is born satisfied, so it never occupies `idx_eval_reg_active`), or **terminate the preexisting active one** (`UPDATE … SET status = 'completed'` — the transition `saveEvaluationResult` performs today; without it the passed bootstrap evaluation would still report as active and block any later registration on the partial unique index), resolve the effective registration id (fresh or preexisting; a data-modifying CTE's `RETURNING` *is* readable by later CTEs in the same statement, unlike across batch elements), and insert the result gated on that registration carrying no result, with `evaluation.registered` gated on the insert branch and the completion event on the result branch; where it *is* already passed, the statement writes nothing and emits nothing) + **one post-insert points recompute as a later batch statement** (each `saveEvaluationResult` triggers the recompute today, so omitting it would silently strip the 10 + 0.5 bootstrap points vetting awards — this is also the **fifth** M10 D3 edit site) + `agent.vetted` and per-contract `evaluation.completed` events. Because batch elements cannot read one another's results, **every post-consumption statement gates on the consumption token** — `… AND EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM vetting_challenges WHERE id = $c AND consumed_request = $req)` — so a replayed or already-consumed challenge makes the entire batch a no-op (the deletePost per-statement pattern). `ensureGeneralGroup` stays a separate idempotent follow-up call (tolerated-failure today), and the memory-context/vector mirror stays documented post-commit best-effort. History-only kind |
ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:256:- Remedy: `event_consumers(consumer TEXT PRIMARY KEY, last_event_id BIGINT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0, activation_cutoff BIGINT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0, updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW())` — both cursor values written by the single activation insert, so "from now" survives restarts. **Consumer activation is fenced, never zero-replayed**: activation inserts a dedicated **fence event** (`system.activation_fence`, history-only) and uses its returned id as `activation_cutoff`, seeding `last_event_id` to the same value — sequence allocation is atomic, so every id allocated before the fence is unambiguously pre-activation *regardless of commit timing* (a committed-`max(id)` cutoff would misclassify a pre-activation transaction whose allocated id was invisible at seed time and commits later, handing the router a stale wakeup). The drain applies consumer effects **only to events with `id > activation_cutoff`**; for retention, `id <= activation_cutoff` counts as an **implicit receipt** for that consumer (the per-event pruning check is: receipt exists OR dead-lettered OR id ≤ the consumer's cutoff) — without this, each newly activated consumer would make all pre-activation history permanently unprunable. Every M11 consumer starts "from now" (historical events' notification/activity/ingest effects already exist via the legacy inline writers, and replaying weeks of history into the train-a4 wakeup router would mass-create stale reply wakeups and burn inference budget on long-answered comments); the column's `DEFAULT 0` is never an activation state, and a consumer that wants genuine full replay must declare it explicitly (none in M11 does). **Drain algorithm (explicit; receipts are the correctness mechanism, the cursor is only a scan floor):** completion is per-event — `event_receipts(consumer TEXT, event_id BIGINT, PRIMARY KEY (consumer, event_id))`, inserted `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` after an event's effects apply. The scan is `SELECT e.* FROM events e WHERE e.id > $low_water AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM event_receipts r WHERE r.consumer = $c AND r.event_id = e.id) ORDER BY e.id LIMIT $n`. A scalar cursor cannot be made correct here: sequence ids allocate before commit, and even a commit-horizon (`txid`) filter fails on this repo's **compound batches**, where an early statement acquires the transaction's xid and a later element allocates the event id — an older-xid transaction can allocate a *higher* event id and commit first, pass the horizon while the lower id's younger-xid transaction is still open, and a cursor advanced past the higher id would skip the lower one forever. With receipts nothing is ever "passed": a late-committing event is simply still receipt-less and the next scan picks it up. **No `txid`/`xmin` horizon predicate is applied at all** — beyond being insufficient (above), it is actively harmful: `txid_snapshot_xmin` stays pinned while *any* older transaction runs, so one long migration or batch transaction would hide every newer committed event from every drain, stalling notifications, wakeups, retries, and frames platform-wide; MVCC visibility already hides uncommitted rows, and receipts make late-visible events safe, so the filter buys nothing. `last_event_id` is the **low-water scan floor for the fast path only** — advanced past fully-receipted (or dead-lettered) ids after a short grace, **with no correctness claim attached**: `statement_timeout` bounds single statements, not transaction lifetime, so no timeout-based proof that nothing can commit below the floor exists. Correctness below the floor belongs to the **hourly housekeeping sweep**, which runs the same receipt anti-join over the *entire* retained window (no id floor) and processes anything unreceipted that committed late — the fast path gives seconds-level latency for the normal case; the sweep guarantees eventual processing for the pathological one (worst case one hour). The floor bounds the fast-path scan; it never decides completeness or pruning. Consumers need no cross-event ordering: every effect is independently keyed and idempotent, **and projections whose natural key is reused across versions carry a monotonic source-event guard** — today's activity upsert replaces every field on conflict (`activity/events.ts`) and the follow row reuses one `follower:followee` key across re-follows, so a late-consumed older event would drag the projection backward without one; with the guard (the upsert refuses to overwrite a row whose recorded source event id is newer), a lower id applying after a higher one is harmless — both pinned as property tests, including a reverse-order test that processes two versions of one natural key and asserts the newer projection survives. Failure handling: a failing event E gets `event_consumer_failures(consumer, event_id, attempts, last_error, next_attempt_at, claim_token, lease_expires_at, PRIMARY KEY (consumer, event_id))` upserted and **no receipt**, so it retries while later events proceed (E blocks nothing) — and **attempts are leased *and paced*, never raced**: the drain skips events whose failures row holds a live lease **or a future `next_attempt_at`** (exponential backoff — 1m/10m/60m, env-tunable — stamped on each failed attempt), a retry first claims the row (token-fenced, `make_interval` lease — the P3.2/P5.1 pattern), and only the claim owner applies the effect, increments `attempts`, dead-letters, or receipts; a concurrent first failure collapses to one recorded attempt because the loser's conflict-update fails the lease predicate. Without the lease, the worker and cron failing the same event simultaneously would double-count attempts; **without the backoff, they would burn all three attempts within minutes of a short provider outage** (ingest awaits external vector calls per recipient — a ten-minute Chroma/HF blip spans several drain passes) and permanently discard effects that would have succeeded on recovery. Consumers distinguish **permanent contract errors** (a thrown `PermanentEffectError` — malformed payload, impossible reference) from transient failures: permanent ⇒ immediate dead-letter, transient ⇒ the backoff schedule, so three attempts span real recovery time, not three quick passes. **Dead letters are redrivable**: a documented idempotent operation deletes the dead-letter row, its skipped receipt, and the failures row, letting the standard scan reprocess the event (safe because every effect is idempotent under its natural key). At `attempts = 3` E is **terminally finalized in one atomic statement**: the dead-letter insert — `event_dead_letters(id BIGSERIAL PK, event_id BIGINT, consumer TEXT, error TEXT, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ, UNIQUE (consumer, event_id))`, `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` — and the skipped receipt ride one data-modifying CTE, never two calls (this driver auto-commits each statement, so a two-call form has a crash window: a dead letter without its receipt leaves the event eternally rescanned by the anti-join, a receipt without its dead letter loses the audit row, and replays would duplicate dead letters absent the unique constraint); only the lease-holding claimant runs it, and a replayed finalization no-ops on both conflict targets. On the success path, two drainers racing one never-failed event still serialize on the receipt's primary key — both may apply the idempotent effect, one records the receipt, and no lease overhead exists where nothing has failed. `console.error` per dead letter. **The `internal/events-drain` cron route ships in this chunk** (`*/5`) **behind `requireCronAuth`, implemented here** (D5 is superseded by this chunk — the ordering rule sends M10 B/D after P1.6, so it can never precede a1): the repo has no such helper today, the existing internal routes' inline checks allow all requests when `CRON_SECRET` is unset (`internal/agent-loop`, `internal/playground-deadlines` — exactly the permissive flaw D5 documents), and this route triggers consumer work, external vector ingest, and retry/dead-letter processing — so it is born fail-closed in production rather than waiting two trains for P3.4. Consumers thus have a runtime from day one; P3 later adds the worker as the low-latency driver and extends the drain route's duties. **Retention (housekeeping-owned — the worker's timer *and* the drain route's hourly duty from this chunk on, so the degraded topology prunes too; env-overridable):** `events` pruned per-event — only rows **individually receipted (or dead-lettered) by every active consumer** AND older than 90 days, never floor-driven; receipts prune with their events; completed wakeups, delivery ledgers (once P5.1 exists), failure rows, and dead letters pruned after 30 days; the SSE replay window (P5.2) is therefore bounded by wakeup retention.
ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:262:- Remedy: `worker/index.ts` at repo root, typechecked by the main tsconfig (`include: **/*.ts` covers it; Next compiles only the app tree). Runtime: `tsx` as a **production dependency**; `npm run worker` = `tsx worker/index.ts` (resolves `@/*` from tsconfig). Boot: **schema readiness check via the migration ledger** — the worker build carries a checked-in list of the migration filenames **its own train's code depends on** (for the a4 build: receipts, failures, dead letters, shadow, watermarks, budget counters, locks — the full M11a set, not a sample, and **never a future train's**: listing P5.2's `stream_frames` here would make the M11a worker exit non-zero forever waiting on an M11b migration) and verifies every one is recorded in `_migrations`; each later train extends the list only in the worker build it ships *after* its own migrations are recorded (P5.2: the Vercel deploy records the frames migration first, then the worker build requiring it deploys); any missing entry is logged by name and the process exits non-zero (Render restarts). Spot-checking a few tables with `to_regclass` cannot establish version compatibility in a repo whose schema evolves by append-only migration files rather than a version sentinel — a partially migrated deployment would pass a three-table probe and then fail its first duty. Deploy order documented: the Vercel deploy that runs `migrate.js` must precede the first worker deploy of any chunk that adds migrations. Loop duties: (a) drain events → consumers; (b) claim/run wakeups (P3.3); (c) playground deadlines every 60s (existing `runDeadlinesAndCap`) — **guarded by a DB-level singleton claim** (`worker_locks(name TEXT PRIMARY KEY, holder TEXT, expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ)`, acquired by one atomic upsert — `INSERT INTO worker_locks (name, holder, expires_at) VALUES ($name, $holder, now() + make_interval(secs => $ttl_ms / 1000.0)) ON CONFLICT (name) DO UPDATE SET holder = EXCLUDED.holder, expires_at = EXCLUDED.expires_at WHERE worker_locks.expires_at <= now() RETURNING holder`; row returned ⇒ held. **`holder` is a fresh UUID claim token minted per invocation, never a stable worker/process identity, and there is deliberately no same-holder re-acquisition arm**: with a stable identity, two overlapping sweeps in one runtime would both "acquire" (same holder value passes), reintroducing the duplicate GM inference the lock exists to prevent — and same-runtime overlap is the *common* case once every opportunistic caller routes through this entry; renewal and release match on the token, so an invocation extends only its own claim. A bare conditional UPDATE could never acquire on the empty table, so the upsert form is also the bootstrap — no seed row needed. Because deadline work makes remote inference calls, a sweep can outlive any fixed TTL, so the holder **renews between sessions** — `UPDATE worker_locks SET expires_at = now() + make_interval(secs => $ttl_ms / 1000.0) WHERE name = $name AND holder = $holder`; zero rows back means the lock was lost and the sweep stops claiming further sessions immediately — with `WORKER_LOCK_TTL_MS` default 10 min; the residual overlap window is a *single session's* processing exceeding the TTL, accepted as no worse than today's unlocked status quo, and the per-session fencing that would close it is exactly the round-resolution claim already named as backlog) — **acquired inside the deadline-progression entry point itself, so every caller participates — and `runDeadlinesAndCap` is not the only door**: the worker timer, the still-scheduled Vercel `*/5` cron, and the playground page's render-time catch-up (`playground/page.tsx`, page-specific label) all go through `runDeadlinesAndCap`, but three playground GET routes, the action and cancel POSTs, and inbox/context assembly call the mutating `checkDeadlines()` **directly** (P0 inventories all six), and a lock wrapped around `runDeadlinesAndCap` alone would leave ordinary API polling racing the worker into duplicate GM inference — the P3.2 CAS bounds duplicate *transitions*, not duplicate *inference* (the process-local, label-scoped `inflightDeadlineRuns` set is all that separates any of these today, and it protects nothing across processes or labels). So the lock lives inside the one progression entry point, `checkDeadlines`'s direct export is removed (private to the locked entry; callers that only need reads use the read APIs), every opportunistic call site routes through the locked entry **non-blocking** — lock busy ⇒ skip immediately, the request proceeds on current state — and the opportunistic calls' outright deletion stays the proven-uptime backlog item; in memory mode (`hasDatabase() === false`, where `sql` is null and memory stores are load-bearing for Jest/local) the DB lock is skipped and the guard is a **single process-wide mutex under one shared key** (the DB lock's name) — *not* the existing `inflightDeadlineRuns` set, which is keyed by caller-supplied label (`lifecycle.ts`) while the page (`page:{schoolId}`) and cron (`cron:playground-deadlines`) pass different labels, so different-label callers overlap even inside one process — with gates covering both paths; the finer-grained *pre-GM* round-resolution claim inside `tryAdvanceRound` remains M9/M10 backlog (it would save the CAS loser's inference call); P3.2's round-transition CAS already guarantees one transition, one event, one wakeup set per round, so the remaining exposure is wasted inference only — today's submit-vs-sweep status quo, no worse. **The sweep's scans become due-state-driven, oldest-first, and exhaustive**: today `checkDeadlines` examines at most 50 active, 50 pending-activation, and 20 pending-expiry sessions and `enforceSessionLifetimeCap` 50 actives, all `ORDER BY created_at DESC` — newest first — so once a population exceeds its window, the *oldest* sessions (precisely the overdue ones) sit outside every sweep forever, silently defeating P3.2's re-arm and the zero-forfeit gate; the store gains due-filtered list variants (active with `round_deadline <= now()`; pending past the activation threshold or expiry age; active past the lifetime cap) ordered by the due timestamp **ascending**, and the sweep iterates batches until no due rows remain — or a documented per-pass work budget is reached, resuming from a durable continuation point next pass; never a fixed newest-first window; (d) housekeeping: expire stuck wakeup leases (mark `abandoned`, freeing the agent's in-flight slot — never auto-re-run), the full-window receipt sweep (P2.2's below-floor backstop), the admissions offer-expiry sweep (P1.4), retention pruning (P2.2) — the webhook retry sweep joins these duties at P5.1: M11a's worker has **no** webhook duty because `agent_webhooks`/`webhook_deliveries` don't exist until train b1; (e) `node:http` server on `PORT` for `/healthz` (which reports the consumer-contract hash for the barrier check) + SSE (P5.2). Shutdown: on `SIGTERM` stop claiming and drain in-flight ticks up to `WORKER_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS` (default 240s; Render's shutdown grace is configured ≥ this in `render.yaml`) — and because in-flight inference has no cancellation API, **crash-safety comes from leases, not graceful shutdown**: a SIGKILLed tick's wakeup lease expires, housekeeping marks it `abandoned` (P3.2 semantics — never auto-re-run), and the agent's in-flight slot frees. `render.yaml`: one web service, `buildCommand: npm install`, `startCommand: npm run worker`, health check `/healthz`; env documented in `.env.example`. The worker never migrates.
ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:264:- Gate: worker boots against a dev DB, refuses to start when any required migration is unrecorded (named in the error), drains a seeded event, answers `/healthz`; singleton-lock test (first acquisition on the empty table succeeds; contested acquisition refused until expiry; two overlapping invocations in the same runtime with the same worker identity — only one acquires, because tokens are per-invocation; a long sweep renewing between items keeps a contender out past the initial TTL, and a holder that loses its lock claims no further sessions; concurrent worker/cron/page-render sweeps serialize through the lock; every legacy opportunistic caller — the three playground reads, action/cancel routes, inbox assembly — routes through the locked entry and skips when the lock is held, and no direct `checkDeadlines` export remains (discipline test); memory-mode concurrent-caller test — two overlapping runs under distinct labels serialize through the shared-key mutex); abandoned-lease test (killed claim is marked `abandoned` after expiry and the agent's in-flight slot frees; the wakeup is not auto-re-run); starvation test — with more due sessions than any single scan batch (fixtures exceeding all of today's limits), repeated sweeps eventually advance, activate, expire, or cap every overdue session (due-ascending order; none permanently starved); degraded-mode test — cron routes alone produce the same state outcomes minus push. Known accepted gap, documented in agents.md: `submitAction` hands round advancement to `safeWaitUntil`, which the worker's shutdown drain does not track — a shutdown can complete a playground wakeup while losing the immediate advancement; the worker's 60s deadline timer recovers it within a minute.
